WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.430
Hey guys, Jared again. Today we're
talking about your favorite little town

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:09.260
in Colorado, yup, South Park.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.600
For the past three seasons, the show's ditched 
its signature standalone episodes, and embraced

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.580
season-long serialization. 
And it's largely been awesome.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.140
Season 19 brilliantly blended themes of PC culture,
advertising, and gentrification, over a

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.880
multi-episode arc. And most recently
season 20 kicked off with an even more

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.840
ambitious storyline that juggled the
presidential election, member berries, a

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.620
militant feminist Cartman, a girl hating
dick flashing Butters,

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.880
and a whole lot of internet trolling.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.220
It all seemed to be leading up to some epic conclusion,

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:46.060
and then, everything just fizzled out into confusion, convulsion, and holy s**t

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.560
they just blew up the internet? 
So why did season 20 leave us feeling all itchy

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.840
and uncertain like a fourth grade
school-wide outbreak of lice had just surfaced?

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.780
Well we have an idea, and it all
has to do with Matt Trey's secret sauce

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.500
that makes South Park's writing so good.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.800
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition of South Park Season 20: What Went Wrong?

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.080
and as always spoilers ahead.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.740
Watching South Park feels like peering inside the
mind of the world's funniest stoner.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.060
Its unpredictable, imaginative. and completely
unhinged in the best way possible.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.880
But, make no mistake, there's a strict
order to the madness.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.020
The show is constructed with one rigid plot line rule:

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.860
We've found out this really simple rule that 
maybe you guys have all heard before.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.060
We can take these beats which are basically 
the beats of your outline

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:37.420
and if the words and then belong between those beats, you're f**ked.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.080
For instance, here's a
three plot storyline about stick figure John.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.860
John adopts a stray dog, and then
John wins the lottery, and then John

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.600
becomes a Scientologist. John's story has
a sucky narrative because there is no

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.920
cause and effect. You could rearrange
each action into any order, and it would

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.390
remain a random series of events in the
life of a lottery winning, dog rescuing,

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.120
intro-level Scientologist. So what makes
good South Park approved writing?

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.200
And whenever you can replace your ands with
buts or therefores makes for better writing.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.940
Take South Park's famous Mormon episode.
An annoyingly perfect Mormon kid moves

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.550
to town, therefore he pisses everyone off,

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:20.560
therefore stan decides to kick his ass.
But the kid is so nice, that he tell Stan

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.240
to go ahead and beat him up.
Therefore Stan doesn't beat him up.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.520
Instead, he agrees to go to the kids house
for dinner. Notice how each action that

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.740
begins with therefore or but indicates a
consequence or reaction to the previous action.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.760
Every plot point matters, which is
why the show never loses momentum.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.560
Okay, so that's South Park's Golden Rule,
but before we dive into what went wrong

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.739
with season 20, it's time to talk about
the orange-tinted

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:49.150
elephant in the room; yup ,the President
of the United States. Stone and Parker

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:53.739
have been upfront about the fact that,
like 99% of the pollsters, they expected

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.140
turd sandwich to win the election, and
planned the series around that outcome. They

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:03.100
had to do desperate last-minute rewrites
in order to air an episode the day after the election.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.360
So, not ideal. As soon as
giant douche Mr. Garrison wins the

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.600
election, the show starts breaking the
creators core rules-

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.340
And f**king them all to death!

